def lbs(nums):
  n = len(nums)
  longest_increasing = [1] * n
  longest_decreasing = [1] * n

  # Calculate longest increasing subsequence lengths
  for i in range(1, n):
    for j in range(i):
      if nums[i] > nums[j]:
        longest_increasing[i] = max(longest_increasing[i], longest_increasing[j] + 1)

  # Calculate longest decreasing subsequence lengths
  for i in range(n-2, -1, -1):
    for j in range(i+1, n):
      if nums[i] > nums[j]:
        longest_decreasing[i] = max(longest_decreasing[i], longest_decreasing[j] + 1)

  # Find the length of the longest bitonic subsequence

  max_lbs = 0
  for i in range(n):
    lbs = longest_increasing[i] + longest_decreasing[i] - 1
    if lbs > max_lbs:
      max_lbs = lbs

  return max_lbs
```
The function `lbs` takes an array `nums` as input and returns the length of the longest bitonic subsequence of `nums`. It uses DP to calculate the lengths of the longest increasing subsequence (`longest_increasing`) and the longest decreasing subsequence (`longest_decreasing`) for each element in `nums`. Then, it finds the maximum of these lengths to determine the length of the longest bitonic subsequence.

