    if n < 3:
        return 0
    # Initialize the first few possible sums with the first three elements
    sums = [arr[i] + arr[i+1] + arr[i+2] for i in range(n-2)]

    # Extend the max sum by considering the next possible three elements
    for end in range(3, n):
        sums.append(max(sums[end-3] + arr[end], sums[end-2] + arr[end], sums[end-1] + arr[end]))

    # Return the maximum sum found
    return max(sums)
```

This Python function `max_sum_of_three_consecutive` calculates the maximum sum of any three consecutive elements in the list `arr` of length `n`. It first initializes an array `sums` with the sum of the three consecutive elements from the start. Then, it iterates over the list, updating the `sums` array with the maximum value of the sum of the last three elements seen so far. Finally, it returns the maximum value from the `sums` array.

