                    dp[i][j][k] = dp[i - 1][j - 1][k - 1] + 1
                else:
                    dp[i][j][k] = max(dp[i - 1][j][k], dp[i][j - 1][k], dp[i][j][k - 1])

    # The maximum value in the table will be the length of the LCS
    return dp[n1][n2][n3]
```

The provided function `lcs_of_three` uses dynamic programming to find the length of the longest common subsequence (LCS) for three given strings. It initializes a 3D list `dp` to store the lengths of LCS of prefixes of the three strings. The function then iterates through each character of the strings, updating the `dp` table based on whether the characters match. finally, it returns the maximum value in the `dp` table, which represents the length of the LCS of the entire three strings.

```python
def lcs_of_three(X, Y, Z, m, n, p):
    # Declaration of a 3D table to store length of longest common subsequence
    tbl = [[[0 for k in range(p+1)] for j in range(n+1)] for i in range(m+1)]
    for i in range(m+1):
        for j in range(n+1):
            for k in range(p+1):
                # Base cases
                if i == 0 or j == 0 or k == 0:
                    tbl[i][j][k] = 0
                elif X[i-1] == Y[j-1] and X[i-1] == Z[k-1]:
                    tbl[i][j][k] = tbl[i-1][j-1][k-1] + 1
                else:
                    tbl[i][j][k] = max(tbl[i-1][j][k],
                                       tbl[i][j-1][k],
                                       tbl[i][j][k-1])
    return tbl[m][n][p]
```

